---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site, description } from '../../content';

const path = '/guides/quiet-hours';
const title = 'Quiet Hours & Timezone SMS Sending';
const desc = description(site, 'guides/quiet-hours');


---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16 prose-shopify">
    <a href="/guides" class="link">← All SMS guides</a>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p>A timezone-aware scheduler must decide whether a message can still be useful when it becomes eligible to send. For Shopify operators, the difficult cases are delayed carts, expiring offers and unknown recipient locations. This guide describes a proposed operating design, not a tested app configuration.</p>
    <h2>Define the applicable window before configuring a clock</h2>
    <p>The US federal rule in <a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-47/chapter-I/subchapter-B/part-64/subpart-L/section-64.1200">47 CFR 64.1200(c)(1)</a> restricts covered telephone solicitations before 8 a.m. or after 9 p.m. at the recipient's location; paragraph (e) addresses wireless texts. That is not a universal permission window for every marketing text.</p>
    <p>Have the person responsible for legal review approve a rule matrix by destination and message purpose. Include state restrictions, applicable exemptions and provider requirements, then choose an operating window inside the applicable limits. Category norms cannot override a legal restriction. A US rule is not a global schedule.</p>
    <p>Record the rule source, effective date, reviewer and configuration version. Distinguish a legal requirement from a stricter brand preference. When either changes, identify queued messages governed by the old version before releasing them.</p>
    <h2>Store the timezone decision and its uncertainty</h2>
    <p>Keep a named timezone, how it was obtained and when it was last refreshed. A fixed UTC offset cannot represent future daylight-saving changes. A shipping address may belong to a gift recipient; a phone area code may reflect a previous home. Neither should silently become verified current location.</p>
    <p>Define an explicit unknown-location branch. Hold the promotional text until the location can be resolved, or use a reviewed conservative intersection of plausible windows. Document the populations that intersection covers; do not label a default store timezone as the customer's local time. If available information conflicts, flag the record instead of choosing whichever source lets the campaign send sooner.</p>
    <h2>Treat delay as a state transition</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>At enrollment, attach event identity, intended purpose, offer expiry and the maximum useful age for that particular message.</li>
      <li>Calculate the next eligible instant using recipient location and the approved rule version. Store the reason for deferral.</li>
      <li>Immediately before dispatch, recheck consent, suppression, location and applicable window. A scheduled job is not permanent send authorization.</li>
      <li>Recheck the business event: has the cart converted, stock disappeared, or the discount expired? Cancel obsolete messages instead of moving them mechanically into the morning.</li>
      <li>Release with recipient-level deduplication and pacing. Avoid turning overnight queues into several competing messages at opening time.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>For an abandoned cart, the useful age depends on the purchase cycle and offer terms. For a launch with a fixed closing time, delay may remove the entire opportunity. Set those policies per flow; a single expiry across all automations hides different customer promises. Do not bypass quiet hours by renaming a promotional reminder as transactional.</p>
    <h2>Proposed boundary checks before enabling a flow</h2>
    <p>These checks have not been executed here. Use controlled recipients and inspect scheduled time, actual handoff time and cancellation reason. Test immediately before opening, at opening, at closing and immediately after closing according to the configured boundary semantics. Repeat across a daylight-saving transition and a timezone that does not change clocks.</p>
    <p>Queue a cart before the window closes, then simulate purchase before reopening: the expected business outcome is cancellation. Repeat with an expired offer, an opt-out during the delay, a changed timezone and a retry after a provider timeout. The retry must use current eligibility and the same event identity. Ask the supplier whether quiet hours govern API calls, manual sends and retries as well as its own flow builder.</p>
    <h2>Inspect delays without mistaking them for failures</h2>
    <p>Report messages deferred, cancelled as stale, held for unknown location and handed off outside the configured window. Keep the scheduled instant, dispatch instant, timezone evidence and rule version together so an operator can explain a complaint. A successful provider handoff is not proof of handset delivery time.</p>
    <p>Compare conversion by event age within eligible cohorts rather than comparing a delayed cohort with every daytime shopper. If many offers expire before release, change offer design or channel strategy. Email fallback still needs its own permission and suppression checks; it should not be an automatic workaround for an SMS hold.</p>
    <p>Use the <a href="/guides/tcpa">TCPA guide</a> for evidence and revocation, <a href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">channel coordination</a> for competing journeys, and the <a href="/apps">app catalog</a> to shortlist senders whose actual queue behavior you can evaluate. No product ranking establishes legal compliance.</p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
